## How the Program Works (Step‑by‑Step)

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Argument parsing** | Checks that at least two arguments (`file` + `word`) exist. Optional `-i` toggles case‑insensitivity. | Guarantees the program has the data it needs and provides a convenient flag. |
| **Open file** | Uses `std::ifstream`. If opening fails, prints an error and exits. | Prevents crashes when the file cannot be read. |
| **Iterate lines** | `while (std::getline(...))` reads the file one line at a time. | Memory usage grows only with the longest line, not with the whole file size. |
| **Split into tokens** | `std::istringstream` with `operator>>` automatically discards any whitespace between words. | Simple and portable way to obtain words. |
| **Trim punctuation** | Removes leading/trailing characters that are *not* alphanumeric (`isalnum`). | Allows “the,” “the.” or “(the)” to be counted as “the”. |
| **Comparison** | If case‑insensitive, both the token and the target are lower‑cased before comparison. | Gives the user the “ignore case” option without extra libraries. |
| **Count** | Increment a counter whenever a match is found. | This is the core metric we want. |
| **Output** | Prints a friendly message that includes the total count. | User‑friendly result. |

