## Extending the Program

If you need more powerful tokenisation (e.g., handling hyphens, apostrophes, Unicode, etc.), replace the `split_into_words`/punctuation‑trimming block with a regular‑expression based splitter:

```cpp
#include <regex>
static std::vector<std::string> split_regex(const std::string& line)
{
    static const std::regex word_regex(R"(\b[\w']+\b)");
    std::vector<std::string> words;
    std::sregex_iterator it(line.begin(), line.end(), word_regex);
    std::sregex_iterator end;
    for (; it != end; ++it)
        words.emplace_back(it->str());
    return words;
}
```

Just call `split_regex(line)` instead of `split_into_words(line)`.

---

## Edge Cases & Considerations

* **Empty file** – the program simply reports “0” occurrences.  
* **Word not present** – also reports “0”.  
* **Very large files** – reading line‑by‑line keeps the memory footprint tiny (only one line stored at a time).  
* **Different delimiters** – the current implementation treats any whitespace as a delimiter; if you need a different rule, adjust the `std::istringstream` approach.  
* **Unicode** – `std::isalnum` works with locale‑specific classification; for full Unicode support you would need a library such as ICU or C++20’s `<unicode>` facilities.

---

